{"id": "DfBkzvRIXcL", "title": "Video by feartalkies", "description": "One woman brought her husband and brother back from the dead… but she swapped their heads. 🔔\n\nLast tale's answer: Vikram said King Shudraka was the bravest. Viravara and his family were born to give their lives for their king, but a king who would give his own life for his servants goes further.\n\nVikram & Betaal: The Swapped Heads.\n\nThe parts we couldn't fit in the reel:\n\nThe city was Shobhavati, ruled by King Yasahketu. Every year, on the fourteenth day of the bright half of Ashadha, people came from every part of the land to bathe in the lake beside the goddess's temple. Dhavala, a young man from the village of Brahmasthala, saw Madanasundari there, the daughter of a man named Suddhapata, and went home so lovesick that he stopped eating.\n\nHis father Vimala comforted him: \"Why so despondent about something so easily arranged? Their family is equal to ours in birth, wealth and trade.\" He asked Suddhapata for the girl, and the two were married the very next day. She had fallen in love with Dhavala at first sight, too.\n\nDhavala went into the temple alone, because a sudden devotion came over him. When the three were alive again, they told each other everything that had happened, were happy, thanked the goddess and walked on, until Madanasundari looked closely and saw what she had done.\n\nSo, who is her husband? The man with his face, or the man with his body? 👇\n\n#vikrambetaal #indianhorror #ghoststories #indianfolklore #halloween", "channel": "feartalkies", "uploader_id": "58803293522", "uploader": "Fear Talkies | Indian Horror", "timestamp": 1793161851, "like_count": 944, "comment_count": 22, "http_headers": {"User-Agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/150.0.0.0 Safari/537.36", "Accept": "text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8", "Accept-Language": "en-us,en;q=0.5", "Sec-Fetch-Mode": "navigate", "Ref